WEBVTT

00:01.400 --> 00:04.468
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:04.536 --> 00:07.872
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:08.153 --> 00:11.221
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:11.288 --> 00:14.189
♪ On which we used to rely? ♪

00:14.257 --> 00:18.126
♪ Lucky there's a family guy ♪

00:18.193 --> 00:21.062
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:21.129 --> 00:22.663
♪ All the things that make us ♪

00:22.731 --> 00:24.032
♪ Laugh and cry ♪

00:24.099 --> 00:26.349
♪ He's... a...
Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:35.245 --> 00:37.080
NEWSCASTER:
And we're back with our coverage

00:37.147 --> 00:38.614
of the Quahog
Thanksgiving Day Parade.

00:38.682 --> 00:39.966
Happy Turkey Day, Quahog.

00:40.033 --> 00:41.734
This is Tom Tucker
and Joyce Kinney

00:41.802 --> 00:42.969
live at the parade.

00:43.036 --> 00:45.138
We've got some on-and-off
drizzle here today,

00:45.205 --> 00:46.873
and we're a little w,
as you can see,

00:46.940 --> 00:50.005
because, apparently, there are
no awnings that exist anywhere.

00:50.408 --> 00:51.932
There's your top news
story right there.

00:51.933 --> 00:54.468
Mysterious awning
shortage ravages the Northeast,

00:54.729 --> 00:57.931
but miraculously has not
affected Channels 2 or 6.

00:59.934 --> 01:01.735
No no. Keep the camera
over there.

01:01.803 --> 01:02.770
I want everyone to see it.

01:02.837 --> 01:04.938
This is why we're third.

01:05.006 --> 01:07.274
And here comes our first
float of the day, Tom.

01:07.342 --> 01:09.576
It's the James Woods
High football team

01:09.644 --> 01:11.195
with their
"Not Gay Revue."

01:11.262 --> 01:12.596
(upbeat music plays)

01:12.664 --> 01:14.732
♪ We just won the big game ♪

01:14.799 --> 01:15.966
(snaps three times)

01:16.034 --> 01:17.534
♪ We just won the big game ♪

01:17.585 --> 01:19.219
(snaps three times)

01:19.320 --> 01:20.537
♪ We drilled 'em and banged 'em ♪
(snaps twice)

01:20.588 --> 01:22.122
♪ And plowed 'em a lot ♪

01:22.190 --> 01:23.474
♪ And though that
sounds suggestive ♪

01:23.541 --> 01:25.442
BARITONE:
♪ Let me stress that it's not. ♪

01:25.510 --> 01:27.897
And here comes the Rocky and
Bullwinkle float. (car alarm blaring)

01:28.047 --> 01:30.482
Always a popular sight
here at the par...

01:30.549 --> 01:32.350
S-Somebody want
to handle that?

01:32.418 --> 01:33.518
Does some--
does some--

01:33.586 --> 01:35.353
Does somebody want
to deal with that?

01:35.421 --> 01:37.455
The car alarm,
"what."

01:37.523 --> 01:38.523
You're kidding.

01:38.591 --> 01:39.858
Well, viewers,
to top it off,

01:39.925 --> 01:42.193
that's the Channel 5
news van you're hearing.

01:42.261 --> 01:45.063
Ph-Phil, Phil just open it--
unlock the doors.

01:45.131 --> 01:47.365
You don't have to put
the key in the ignition.

01:47.433 --> 01:49.501
Well, open the
driver's side first.

01:49.568 --> 01:51.503
Okay, well, I guess
it's broken, then,

01:51.570 --> 01:52.871
and there's nothing
we can do about it.

01:52.938 --> 01:54.139
Okay, happy Thanksgiving,
Quahog.

01:54.206 --> 01:55.240
Here's a car alarm.

01:55.307 --> 01:56.524
(alarm continues blaring)
(sighs)

01:56.592 --> 01:57.892
All right, well,
I guess we'll just...

01:57.960 --> 01:59.327
(alarm stops with a tweet)
Ahhh, there we go.

01:59.395 --> 02:00.478
(doorbell rings)

02:00.546 --> 02:01.796
Oh, that must be
Joe and Bonnie.

02:01.881 --> 02:03.798
Now remember everyone,
let's be cheerful,

02:03.866 --> 02:05.934
'cause Thanksgivings
are very hard for them.

02:06.001 --> 02:07.469
'Cause Joe can't walk?
No.

02:07.536 --> 02:09.838
'Cause his bum doesn't
open up to poo anymore,

02:09.905 --> 02:11.806
and a nurse has to
go digging up there

02:11.874 --> 02:13.391
to get up after
his stuff?

02:13.459 --> 02:14.392
(laughs)

02:14.460 --> 02:15.510
Oh, my God!

02:15.578 --> 02:16.611
(laughs)
I know.

02:16.679 --> 02:18.146
That was told to me
in confidence.

02:18.214 --> 02:19.814
No. Don't you
remember, Peter?

02:19.882 --> 02:21.166
It was
on Thanksgiving

02:21.233 --> 02:22.934
that their son Kevin
got killed in Iraq.

02:24.069 --> 02:25.321
Look who's here!

02:25.388 --> 02:26.739
Happy Thanksgiving.

02:26.790 --> 02:27.923
Gobble gobble.

02:27.991 --> 02:30.326
You guys, thanks so much
for having us over.

02:30.393 --> 02:32.928
Well, we're just glad we
can have people over again

02:32.996 --> 02:34.396
now that that giant
crab's gone.

02:34.464 --> 02:36.549
Hey, guys, how ya doin'?
Come on in.

02:36.616 --> 02:38.334
(rapid staccato):
No-no-no-no-no-no-no.

02:38.418 --> 02:39.635
No-no-no-no-no,
no-no-no-no-no.

02:39.719 --> 02:40.786
Just go around it.

02:40.837 --> 02:41.637
He's not gonna
hurt ya.

02:41.721 --> 02:42.922
Just go around it.

02:42.973 --> 02:43.956
No-no-no-no-no-no-no.

02:44.007 --> 02:44.924
No-no-no-no-no,
no-no-no-no-no.

02:44.975 --> 02:46.058
No-no-no-no-no.

02:46.125 --> 02:47.459
All right, try just
going under him.

02:47.511 --> 02:49.061
I think we should
probably just go home.

02:49.128 --> 02:50.346
Maybe we'll do it
another time.

02:50.430 --> 02:51.263
No-no-no-no-no.

02:58.355 --> 02:59.305
What the hell?

02:59.372 --> 03:00.322
Mom!

03:00.407 --> 03:01.407
Mom, it's me! Mom!

03:01.475 --> 03:03.142
Oh, yeah, it's a baby.

03:03.210 --> 03:04.810
It's a big balloon, baby,

03:04.878 --> 03:06.145
just like you're a baby.

03:06.213 --> 03:07.179
No, it's me!

03:07.247 --> 03:08.197
It's a balloon of me!

03:08.265 --> 03:09.582
Peter, come
look at this.

03:09.649 --> 03:12.117
He sees a baby and knows
that he's also a baby.

03:12.185 --> 03:13.352
So smart.

03:13.420 --> 03:15.054
Mama's smart little boy.

03:15.121 --> 03:16.755
Aah, check out Quagmire.

03:16.823 --> 03:18.157
It's been a year and a half,

03:18.225 --> 03:20.509
and he still looks
uncomfortable around his dad.

03:20.577 --> 03:22.145
Well, it's a
big adjustment.

03:22.213 --> 03:25.215
After all, his dad did have
a sex-change operation.

03:25.283 --> 03:27.818
So, uh...
how-how are things, Dad?

03:27.885 --> 03:31.154
Oh, Glenn, I can't tell you how
wonderful it is to be back here.

03:31.222 --> 03:33.473
This is gonna be a really
special Thanksgiving.

03:33.524 --> 03:35.408
Happy Thanksgiving!

03:35.476 --> 03:36.960
We brought
chicken wings.

03:37.028 --> 03:39.729
Oh, yum. I hope they're the
kind with the bone removed.

03:41.365 --> 03:42.465
(doorbell rings)

03:43.868 --> 03:44.818
Hey, Aunt Carol.

03:44.886 --> 03:45.886
Hey, Uncle Adam.

03:45.953 --> 03:47.287
♪ La la la la la. ♪

03:47.355 --> 03:49.489
<i>Don't mention last night's
Project Runway.</i>

03:49.557 --> 03:50.991
I've got it on TiVo.

03:51.042 --> 03:52.325
Happy Thanksgiving.

03:52.376 --> 03:53.977
I brought Marshmallow Peeps.

03:54.045 --> 03:54.978
Oh.

03:55.046 --> 03:56.179
Okay.

03:56.247 --> 03:58.415
I'm gonna need
that Pyrex dish back.

03:58.482 --> 03:59.616
Okay.

03:59.684 --> 04:01.651
I'm gonna need it back now.

04:01.719 --> 04:02.886
Oh.

04:04.255 --> 04:05.805
I don't want
anything else today

04:05.873 --> 04:07.874
to get as tense
as that just got.

04:07.925 --> 04:09.676
Carol, it's so good
to see you.

04:09.744 --> 04:10.977
You, too, Lois.

04:11.045 --> 04:12.646
God, that is
one nice ass.

04:12.713 --> 04:14.080
Thank you.
You're welcome.

04:14.148 --> 04:15.916
(TV playing)

04:15.983 --> 04:17.083
This is nice.

04:17.151 --> 04:18.752
This is very nice,

04:18.819 --> 04:20.888
the time that we're spending
together right now.

04:20.956 --> 04:21.889
Easy, Joe.

04:21.957 --> 04:22.990
(groans)

04:23.058 --> 04:25.026
Sorry.
Just making mental memories

04:25.093 --> 04:27.028
for when you're all
inevitably dead,

04:27.095 --> 04:28.362
and I'm left alone.

04:28.430 --> 04:30.164
Geez. Quagmire, help me out
with this.

04:30.232 --> 04:31.666
No, he's right.
I mean, sometimes

04:31.733 --> 04:33.668
chicks die, and it's
not anyone's fault.

04:33.735 --> 04:36.604
Okay, everyone, it's 2:30.
Time for dinner.

04:36.672 --> 04:40.107
'Cause on Thanksgiving, 2:30
is dinnertime for some reason.

04:43.579 --> 04:44.779
Oh, my.

04:44.846 --> 04:47.148
Lois, those sweet potatoes
look delicious.

04:47.215 --> 04:48.649
That's the
stuffing, Ma.

04:48.717 --> 04:51.452
Now put on your glasses before
you run over another black guy.

04:51.520 --> 04:53.087
Oh, this looks fantastic.

04:53.155 --> 04:55.106
I can't wait to poop this out.

04:55.190 --> 04:56.424
Hey! Who's gonna get

04:56.491 --> 04:58.559
the Thanksgiving dump trophy
this year?

04:58.627 --> 05:00.294
You are!
Dad's gonna get it!

05:00.362 --> 05:01.228
Who else?

05:01.280 --> 05:02.280
(all laughing)

05:02.364 --> 05:04.065
Loving.
Loving family.

05:04.132 --> 05:06.100
Well, dig in, everyone!

05:10.272 --> 05:11.906
Hello, Ida.
Hello, Brian.

05:11.974 --> 05:13.808
How have you been?
Very well, thank you.

05:13.875 --> 05:16.812
He threw up when he found out
you were a monster.

05:16.879 --> 05:19.214
This food is so (bleep) good,
Lois.

05:19.282 --> 05:21.583
Oh. Okay.
Wow.

05:21.651 --> 05:23.919
MAN: Hey! Is there room at that table
for one more?

05:23.987 --> 05:25.487
(all gasp)

05:27.840 --> 05:29.041
Oh, my God!

05:29.108 --> 05:30.609
Kevin Swanson!

05:30.677 --> 05:32.477
Son, you're alive!

05:32.545 --> 05:35.447
Aw, and I stayed up all night
writing dead kid jokes.

05:35.515 --> 05:37.182
All right, you know what,
I'm gonna do one anyway.

05:37.250 --> 05:38.517
Kevin, go back outside.

05:38.601 --> 05:40.068
Everybody pretend
this didn't happen.

05:40.136 --> 05:43.705
Hey, Joe, what's your favorite
preparation of a tomato?

05:43.773 --> 05:46.475
Is it son-died tomato?

05:47.610 --> 05:50.512
Is it son-died tomato?

05:50.580 --> 05:52.147
Okay, Kevin, you
can come back in.

05:52.198 --> 05:53.481
Welcome home, soldier.

05:53.533 --> 05:54.483
(chuckles)

05:54.550 --> 05:56.151
Son-died tomato.

05:56.219 --> 05:56.969
(slide whistle squeaks)

06:03.359 --> 06:04.809
Kevin, you're alive!

06:04.877 --> 06:05.743
I don't believe it.

06:05.795 --> 06:07.812
Oh, my God, it's a miracle!

06:07.880 --> 06:09.647
Well, I guess
you replaced real butter

06:09.715 --> 06:11.015
as the star of the dinner.

06:11.083 --> 06:12.550
But I don't understand.

06:12.618 --> 06:14.320
The Army said you'd
been killed in action.

06:14.387 --> 06:15.287
What happened?

06:15.355 --> 06:18.057
Well, it's quite a story.

06:18.124 --> 06:21.560
<i>In some ways, Army life in Iraq
is what you'd expect,</i>

06:21.628 --> 06:23.295
<i>what with the blistering heat,</i>

06:23.346 --> 06:25.064
<i>the constant sense
of impending danger,</i>

06:25.131 --> 06:26.899
<i>and the one gay soldier</i>

06:26.966 --> 06:28.534
<i>awkwardly avoiding
the use of pronouns.</i>

06:28.602 --> 06:30.936
Man, I sure miss my
sweetheart back home.

06:31.004 --> 06:33.155
I can't wait to get
back to that person.

06:33.223 --> 06:34.824
Chow time, boys.

06:34.908 --> 06:36.625
Yes, sir, Sergeant
Major General Captain!

06:36.693 --> 06:38.460
Ah, come on, boys,
it's Thanksgiving.

06:38.528 --> 06:40.362
Call me Major
General Captain.

06:40.430 --> 06:41.614
Now you eat up.

06:43.133 --> 06:45.835
I don't trust that turkey
those Iraqis made.

06:45.919 --> 06:47.453
Ah, you're all wet, Swanson.

06:47.504 --> 06:48.454
I call the white meat.

06:48.505 --> 06:49.839
I call the dark meat.

06:49.923 --> 06:51.457
I call the cell phone
duct-taped to the side!

06:51.508 --> 06:52.341
Noooooo!

06:56.213 --> 06:58.347
They're all dead, sir,
except for Swanson.

06:58.415 --> 07:00.616
He's in a coma, but he's
unlikely to recover.

07:00.684 --> 07:03.402
Okay, well, just send Swanson's
parents a letter saying

07:03.470 --> 07:05.604
that he's dead, 'cause
those are already printed.

07:05.672 --> 07:07.206
You know what? Send one
to my parents, too.

07:07.274 --> 07:10.209
I spent five years in a
Kuwait military hospital.

07:10.277 --> 07:12.128
I woke up from
the coma last week,

07:12.196 --> 07:13.530
and I flew straight here.

07:13.614 --> 07:15.248
Kind of a
Thanksgiving surprise.

07:15.316 --> 07:18.184
Hey, when you get sexually
abused while you're in a coma,

07:18.252 --> 07:19.669
do you know
it's happening

07:19.737 --> 07:21.121
and just can't do
anything about it?

07:21.188 --> 07:22.756
Or do u not even
know what's going on?

07:22.823 --> 07:24.491
I also am curious about that.

07:24.559 --> 07:26.126
Hey, Kevin, you know
I put a yellow ribbon

07:26.193 --> 07:27.527
on my car for you guys?

07:27.595 --> 07:30.263
And-and a little thing that says
"I support the troops."

07:30.331 --> 07:32.766
'Cause there's nothing
I wouldn't do for you guys.

07:32.833 --> 07:35.168
but I don't have
any change on me right now.

07:35.219 --> 07:37.787
Well, all that matters
is that my boy is home.

07:37.855 --> 07:40.957
Kevin, I want you to meet
your little sister, Susie.

07:41.025 --> 07:41.958
Wow.

07:42.026 --> 07:43.126
Hey, little sis.

07:43.194 --> 07:44.794
I guess we got
some catching up to do, huh?

07:44.862 --> 07:45.962
Hope you
don't get scared

07:46.030 --> 07:47.697
when I scream and
have night terrors.

07:47.765 --> 07:48.832
<i>SUSIE: (├а la Patrick Stewart):
It's quite all right.</i>

07:48.900 --> 07:50.000
I've seen worse.

07:50.067 --> 07:51.201
(horror movie score plays)

07:51.269 --> 07:52.736
(woman screams on TV)

07:52.803 --> 07:54.537
<i>SUSIE: (├а la Patrick Stewart):
A human centipede.</i>

07:54.605 --> 07:55.872
<i>How ghastly.</i>

07:55.940 --> 07:58.341
Your mother and I are
very proud of you, Kevin.

07:58.409 --> 07:59.743
You're a war hero.

07:59.810 --> 08:01.344
I don't feel
like a war hero.

08:01.412 --> 08:03.046
Only a war hero
would say that.

08:03.114 --> 08:05.649
Hey, so what about the female
soldiers over there?

08:05.716 --> 08:06.950
Were they able to contribute?

08:07.018 --> 08:08.603
Eh, you know,
they did their best.

08:08.670 --> 08:10.171
What with their
regular bleedings

08:10.238 --> 08:11.989
when they got shot,
did they even know?

08:12.057 --> 08:13.691
Look, guys,
I'm not a hero.

08:13.759 --> 08:15.876
And I don't want to be treated
any different than before.

08:15.928 --> 08:17.078
Just that you would say that

08:17.145 --> 08:18.746
means we should treat you
different.

08:18.814 --> 08:22.350
Come on, guys, let's give him
all our best food.

08:22.417 --> 08:24.652
Okay, now you eat up, and then
we're all gonna go out back

08:24.720 --> 08:27.488
and play some Thanksgiving touch
football-- excuse me:

08:27.556 --> 08:30.191
war hero Thanksgiving
touch football.

08:35.597 --> 08:36.814
Yeaaaaah!

08:36.898 --> 08:38.115
U.S.A.!

08:38.200 --> 08:39.400
I didn't even know
there was a war.

08:41.936 --> 08:43.337
Okay, let's pick teams.

08:43.405 --> 08:44.639
I'll be a captain,

08:44.706 --> 08:46.891
and, uh, Mayor West,
you can be a captain.

08:46.959 --> 08:48.559
<i>All right.
I pick you.</i>

08:48.627 --> 08:50.845
You can't pick me;
I'm a captain.

08:50.912 --> 08:54.015
No one had ever stood up to me
like that before.

08:54.082 --> 08:56.167
I respected him
from there on out.

08:56.234 --> 08:57.668
That's a great
story, Grandpa.

08:57.736 --> 08:59.220
I didn't like it.

08:59.287 --> 09:00.655
None of my grandchildren

09:00.722 --> 09:03.624
had ever disliked one
of my stories before.

09:03.692 --> 09:06.228
From then on,
he was my favorite.

09:06.295 --> 09:08.196
Well, I don't like that
story, Great Grandpa.

09:08.264 --> 09:09.614
(crackling)

09:09.682 --> 09:12.250
Future old people
are wizards.

09:12.318 --> 09:13.986
(slurred):
Okay, so here's the game:

09:14.053 --> 09:15.354
I'm... I'm drunk,

09:15.421 --> 09:18.757
and I'm gonna throw the football
too hard at my kids.

09:18.825 --> 09:20.275
Oh, Dad, do we have to--
Ow!

09:20.326 --> 09:22.310
Don't cry!
Don't cry!

09:22.362 --> 09:23.862
Game's over if you cry.

09:23.930 --> 09:26.765
I want you to learn
about life, Chris,

09:26.833 --> 09:29.101
'cause of, in life
it's like this.

09:30.470 --> 09:32.604
Peter, I think you may
have had one too many.

09:32.672 --> 09:34.506
Maybe it's time
to ease off, huh?

09:34.574 --> 09:35.841
What are you talking
about, Lois?

09:35.908 --> 09:37.509
Dads getting drunk
on Thanksgiving

09:37.577 --> 09:38.944
is a holiday tradition.

09:39.012 --> 09:42.080
(slurring): I work hard to put
a cornucopia on this table,

09:42.148 --> 09:44.182
and you're out
showing your lower ankle

09:44.250 --> 09:47.436
to every Tom, Tom, and
other Tom in our village!

09:47.503 --> 09:49.454
Don't talk to mother
in like fashion!

09:49.522 --> 09:51.773
That's it.
The belt is coming off.

09:55.812 --> 09:56.962
All right, son.

09:57.030 --> 09:59.131
Let's put these bozos
in a hurt locker.

09:59.198 --> 10:01.199
Right? 'Cause, uh,
"hurt locker" means

10:01.267 --> 10:02.969
some kind of war hero
thing, or something.

10:03.037 --> 10:04.704
Pfft. My friends
and I used to laugh

10:04.772 --> 10:06.139
about how fake
that movie is.

10:06.206 --> 10:07.140
But...

10:07.207 --> 10:08.558
Well, wait, I, uh...

10:08.625 --> 10:10.693
I'm pretty sure
that movie came out

10:10.761 --> 10:12.462
after you went
into your coma.

10:12.529 --> 10:14.897
Right-- no, no, uh, I-I-I was
talking about a different movie.

10:14.965 --> 10:15.948
Let's play ball!

10:16.016 --> 10:19.085
Yeah, let's play ball.

10:19.153 --> 10:20.903
(ball pinging off someone's
skin, howls of pain)

10:20.971 --> 10:22.805
PETER (slurred):
The world has changed around me,

10:22.873 --> 10:24.373
and I'm mad and scared!

10:28.479 --> 10:31.214
Lois, this pumpkin pie
looks delightful.

10:31.281 --> 10:33.199
Hey, Kevin, can I see
your Purple Heart?

10:33.267 --> 10:34.700
'Cause I never
actually seen one.

10:34.768 --> 10:37.370
One-one time this homeless guy
showed me his purple head,

10:37.438 --> 10:39.705
but looking back,
I'm-I'm not so sure

10:39.773 --> 10:41.207
that that was really
a military thing.

10:41.275 --> 10:43.009
Pfft. I didn't want
that stupid medal.

10:43.077 --> 10:44.343
You know what I
did with it?

10:44.411 --> 10:46.012
I threw it back over
the White House fence.

10:46.080 --> 10:47.513
Wait a minute.
I thought you said

10:47.581 --> 10:49.115
you flew straight
home from Kuwait

10:49.183 --> 10:50.750
after you woke
from your coma.

10:50.818 --> 10:53.519
When did you throw your medal
over the White House fence?

10:53.587 --> 10:56.355
N-N-N-No, I-I-I told you, I flew
to D.C., ditched the medals,

10:56.423 --> 10:57.623
and flew home.

10:57.691 --> 10:59.827
I guess that was just
his dirty hobo penis, huh?

10:59.894 --> 11:01.595
Kevin, why would you
want to get rid of

11:01.663 --> 11:02.763
your war medals
like that?

11:02.831 --> 11:04.465
'Cause it was
a stupid war, you know?

11:04.532 --> 11:07.551
All it did was put me in a coma
and kill one of my bunkmates

11:07.619 --> 11:09.319
and cripple the other one
for life.

11:09.387 --> 11:10.721
W-Wait a minute.

11:10.789 --> 11:12.089
Now, I thought
earlier you said

11:12.157 --> 11:13.791
that both of your
bunkmates were killed.

11:13.858 --> 11:16.260
Now you're saying
only one of them was?

11:16.327 --> 11:17.795
But I just want
you guys to know

11:17.862 --> 11:19.696
I barely even stared
at that penis for an hour.

11:19.764 --> 11:21.165
What? Come on, Dad.

11:21.232 --> 11:22.466
Whatev.

11:22.534 --> 11:23.901
H-Hey, why's it
so hot in here?

11:23.952 --> 11:25.085
It's too hot in here.

11:25.153 --> 11:26.120
Wait a minute.

11:26.187 --> 11:27.671
An Ed Hardy T-shirt?

11:27.739 --> 11:29.907
That particular
strain of douche bag

11:29.974 --> 11:31.942
didn't arise
until 2007,

11:32.010 --> 11:33.811
while you were
supposedly in your coma.

11:33.878 --> 11:35.129
Look, get off my case, Dad!

11:35.196 --> 11:36.530
What do you want from me?

11:36.598 --> 11:37.498
The truth.

11:37.565 --> 11:39.099
Fine! Here's the truth!

11:39.167 --> 11:40.934
I was never in any coma, okay?

11:41.002 --> 11:41.935
I bailed.

11:42.003 --> 11:43.737
I faked my death and went AWOL.

11:43.805 --> 11:44.505
(all gasp)

11:44.572 --> 11:46.240
I faked my own death once.

11:50.962 --> 11:52.796
(sobbing)

11:57.086 --> 11:58.937
(dirt crunching)

12:02.341 --> 12:03.474
(chuckles)

12:03.542 --> 12:06.194
No dentist appointment
for this guy.

12:06.262 --> 12:07.395
So, there it is.

12:07.463 --> 12:08.563
I'm not a war hero.

12:08.631 --> 12:09.731
I abandoned my platoon.

12:09.798 --> 12:11.099
So now what do we do?

12:12.301 --> 12:13.868
Me?

12:13.936 --> 12:15.904
Am-Am I, am I supposed
to say something?

12:15.971 --> 12:17.622
Kevin,

12:17.690 --> 12:19.657
you're under arrest for
the crime of desertion.

12:19.725 --> 12:21.109
Oh, my.

12:21.177 --> 12:23.778
Um, maybe it's time for us
girls to hit the powder room.

12:23.846 --> 12:24.812
You may use
the yard.

12:31.783 --> 12:34.318
So you were lying
to us this whole time?

12:34.385 --> 12:36.019
You weren't really
injured in Iraq?

12:36.087 --> 12:39.056
First Pat Tillman lies
about how he died and now you?

12:39.123 --> 12:40.824
Kevin, I don't
understand.

12:40.892 --> 12:42.192
There's nothing to understand.

12:42.260 --> 12:43.794
Our son is a deserter.

12:43.861 --> 12:45.512
Come on, Kevin,
I'm taking you to jail.

12:45.596 --> 12:47.164
Joe, wait!
Not now, Bonnie.

12:47.231 --> 12:48.398
Joe, you can't!

12:48.466 --> 12:49.666
Bonnie, we'll discuss
this later!

12:49.717 --> 12:52.502
Joe! You're not taking
my son anywhere.

12:52.570 --> 12:54.439
Damn it, you guys
are ruining Thanksgiving!

12:54.506 --> 12:56.240
And it's my second
favorite holiday,

12:56.308 --> 12:59.010
right after Pretend Not
To Notice People's Race Day.

12:59.078 --> 13:00.178
Hey, you seen
Reggie around?

13:00.245 --> 13:01.846
Which one is Reggie?

13:01.914 --> 13:04.682
Oh, uh, today he's
wearing yellow pants,

13:04.750 --> 13:08.519
uh, yellow shirt, yellow
hat, yellow shoes.

13:08.587 --> 13:11.422
Uh, he's got a gold tooth,
uh, diamond earring.

13:11.490 --> 13:14.459
Um, he's got that big necklace
with the dollar sign on it.

13:14.526 --> 13:16.694
Uh, he's always
grabbing his crotch

13:16.762 --> 13:17.895
when he's
telling a story.

13:17.963 --> 13:19.130
Like the one about
when he found out

13:19.198 --> 13:20.665
his mother is really
his grandmother.

13:20.733 --> 13:22.133
Could you be more specific?

13:22.201 --> 13:23.601
Uh, oh, let's see.

13:23.669 --> 13:25.803
Uh, he always wears
cheap cologne.

13:25.871 --> 13:29.073
Uh, drives a Mercury Cougar
with a crown on the dashboard.

13:29.141 --> 13:31.042
Uh, never pays
his alimony.

13:31.110 --> 13:33.845
Uh, he's extremely cut for
a guy who never works out.

13:33.912 --> 13:35.263
Oh, there he is.
Thanks anyway.

13:35.347 --> 13:36.347
Hey, Reggie!

13:37.850 --> 13:39.283
Look, maybe we should
just let Kevin

13:39.351 --> 13:41.102
explain why he did what he did.

13:42.237 --> 13:43.337
(sighs)

13:43.405 --> 13:44.989
At first,
I believed in the cause.

13:45.056 --> 13:47.391
But then I saw what we were
doing to the people of Iraq.

13:47.443 --> 13:48.559
Innocent people.

13:48.627 --> 13:50.745
There was one
that I'll never forget.

13:50.813 --> 13:52.915
<i>He was a little
Iraqi street kid.</i>

13:52.999 --> 13:54.900
<i>I'd befriended him
a few months earlier.</i>

13:54.951 --> 13:56.752
<i>Couldn't have been
more than 12 years old.</i>

13:56.836 --> 13:58.937
<i>I taught him how to crack wise,
American style.</i>

14:00.774 --> 14:02.791
(people screaming)

14:02.859 --> 14:05.344
I guess that store's
having a fire sale.

14:05.412 --> 14:07.079
(laughs)

14:07.147 --> 14:09.515
<i>I tell you, that kid was
strong-headed for his age.</i>

14:09.582 --> 14:11.016
<i>Smart, but innocent.</i>

14:11.083 --> 14:13.435
<i>And then one day,
there was a fire fight.</i>

14:23.396 --> 14:25.881
<i>We bring in this civilian who
got killed in the crossfire.</i>

14:27.901 --> 14:30.068
<i>I pull back the sheet,
and who is it?</i>

14:30.136 --> 14:31.620
<i>Not the kid, but, like,</i>

14:31.704 --> 14:34.272
<i>the kid's dad's, like,
coworker's neighbor.</i>

14:34.324 --> 14:37.242
<i>He was 74 and he had actually
tried to rape someone.</i>

14:37.293 --> 14:39.378
<i>But just looking down
at his dead face</i>

14:39.445 --> 14:42.164
<i>and knowing that he lived near
someone who worked with the dad</i>

14:42.248 --> 14:45.701
<i>of the kid whose name I never
found out, I lost all hope.</i>

14:45.769 --> 14:48.086
<i>That's when I knew
I had to get out of Iraq.</i>

14:48.138 --> 14:50.006
<i>I just had to wait
for my chance.</i>

14:50.090 --> 14:51.007
I call the white meat!

14:51.091 --> 14:52.375
I call the dark meat!

14:52.443 --> 14:53.927
I call the cell phone
duct taped to the side!

14:53.978 --> 14:54.844
No!

14:59.350 --> 15:01.568
<i>Somehow, I'd been spared
that Thanksgiving.</i>

15:01.635 --> 15:04.020
<i>But I can't say the same
for my bunkmates.</i>

15:04.104 --> 15:06.022
<i>There were bits and pieces
of them everywhere.</i>

15:06.106 --> 15:08.975
<i>I knew the Army would never be
able to identify who was who.</i>

15:09.026 --> 15:12.245
<i>So that was my chance
to disappear.</i>

15:12.313 --> 15:13.480
So I left.

15:13.547 --> 15:14.814
How could you
do that?!

15:14.865 --> 15:16.032
Coward!
Traitor!

15:16.100 --> 15:18.001
If you think
that I'm gonna masturbate

15:18.068 --> 15:20.370
after this tonight,
you're right.

15:20.454 --> 15:22.272
We don't have to sit
here and listen to this.

15:22.339 --> 15:24.040
Come on, Kevin, let's
get out of here.

15:24.108 --> 15:25.675
Nice try, skank.
Worth a shot.

15:25.759 --> 15:27.961
Well, where did you go
after you left the war?

15:28.012 --> 15:29.846
I spent a few years laying low.

15:29.914 --> 15:31.581
Wrote some poems.
You want to hear some?

15:31.649 --> 15:32.482
No.
Are you sure?

15:32.550 --> 15:33.917
They're pretty intense.

15:33.984 --> 15:35.685
ALL: No.
Here, let me just read one.

15:35.753 --> 15:38.087
If I can guess a line in there,
will you not read it?

15:38.155 --> 15:39.856
I don't think you'll be able
to guess a line.

15:39.924 --> 15:42.826
Is it something like,
"Ice burns as hot as fire"?

15:42.893 --> 15:47.064
Okay, so then I went to Europe,
Australia, Canada, all over.

15:47.132 --> 15:48.949
But I really missed you guys.

15:49.017 --> 15:51.752
I wanted to be with people
who love me, no matter what.

15:51.820 --> 15:53.087
So I came home.

15:53.154 --> 15:54.488
Well, it sounds
to me like

15:54.556 --> 15:56.957
you're a regular
Benedict Arnold Drummond.

15:57.025 --> 15:58.859
W-What's he expecting us
to cut to?

15:58.910 --> 16:00.678
I think he wants maybe--
I don't know--

16:00.745 --> 16:02.012
Gary Coleman
in a Napoleon hat?

16:02.080 --> 16:03.230
But we don't have that.

16:03.298 --> 16:04.364
We-We got to come up
with something.

16:04.416 --> 16:06.133
Well, we got, uh, we got

16:06.201 --> 16:07.768
"The Cowardly Lion is
Lindsay Lohan's gynecologist."

16:07.836 --> 16:08.886
Play it, play it.

16:08.954 --> 16:10.421
But he didn't set it up.
Play it!

16:10.488 --> 16:12.206
All right, I'm gonna
check her for diseases.

16:12.274 --> 16:14.241
There's just one thing
I want you to do.

16:14.309 --> 16:15.175
What's that?
What's that?

16:15.243 --> 16:16.393
Talk me out of it.

16:16.478 --> 16:18.312
TIN MAN and SCARECROW:
No, no, no, no, no, no.

16:18.380 --> 16:20.581
What the hell
is going on up there?

16:20.649 --> 16:22.816
Well, I'm sorry
you all feel the way you do.

16:22.884 --> 16:25.286
But I walked away from
an illegal war of aggression

16:25.353 --> 16:28.055
being fought 6,000 miles away
from our shores.

16:28.123 --> 16:30.090
Better there than here
where all my stuff is.

16:30.158 --> 16:33.077
So, what, you figured you'd just
let 'em get away with 9/11?

16:33.161 --> 16:35.663
Mr. Griffin, Iraq had
nothing to do with 9/11.

16:35.730 --> 16:38.566
And the war we started in Iraq
has killed a half a million

16:38.633 --> 16:41.569
of their people, which
is like 200 9/11's.

16:41.636 --> 16:44.072
So, I guess those moments
of silence we had

16:44.140 --> 16:45.874
at those sporting events
meant nothing to you.

16:45.942 --> 16:47.659
I'm just saying
this war is wrong.

16:47.727 --> 16:51.513
Son, your job isn't to decide
whether it's right or wrong.

16:51.580 --> 16:53.515
Your job is to
follow orders.

16:53.583 --> 16:55.317
Well, that's what the Nazi
soldiers did, isn't it?

16:55.384 --> 16:56.651
They followed orders.

16:56.719 --> 16:58.520
You're saying our troops
are like the Nazis?!

16:58.587 --> 17:01.773
No, I'm just saying that blindly
following orders has resulted

17:01.857 --> 17:04.192
in the deaths of millions
of people throughout history.

17:04.260 --> 17:06.561
You know, I have not talked
to Carol all night.

17:06.629 --> 17:08.096
Carol, how are you?

17:08.164 --> 17:10.632
Look, I think you guys are
all rushing to judge Kevin

17:10.700 --> 17:13.335
without knowing what it was like
to be in his shoes.

17:13.402 --> 17:16.304
He saw horrific things,
in a horrific war,

17:16.372 --> 17:18.373
that only one other person here

17:18.441 --> 17:21.209
could possibly come close
to understanding: Ida.

17:21.277 --> 17:22.444
She was in the military.

17:22.511 --> 17:23.979
She's seen battle.

17:24.046 --> 17:26.681
She's seen innocent people
butchered by war machines.

17:26.749 --> 17:29.217
And I'm sure that Ida,
more than anyone else here,

17:29.285 --> 17:32.287
can understand and support
the choice that Kevin made.

17:32.355 --> 17:35.590
No, Brian, I do not understand
Kevin's choice,

17:35.658 --> 17:37.325
and I do not support it.

17:37.393 --> 17:39.177
Okay, well, that's just
some dumb drag queen.

17:39.245 --> 17:41.814
Son, when you go to war,
you don't end up

17:41.882 --> 17:45.051
fighting for your country
or your family or your flag.

17:45.118 --> 17:46.686
You're fighting for
your fellow soldiers

17:46.753 --> 17:48.754
there in the foxhole with you.

17:48.822 --> 17:52.541
You walked out on those men, and
that's what's most unforgivable.

17:52.609 --> 17:54.343
You know what, everyone?
Forget it.

17:54.411 --> 17:56.145
Up till today,
you guys thought I was dead.

17:56.213 --> 17:57.179
Well, I'm not.

17:57.247 --> 17:58.481
But after all this,

17:58.548 --> 18:00.232
Dad, you may as well
take me to prison,

18:00.300 --> 18:02.134
'cause right now
I wish I was dead.

18:02.202 --> 18:05.438
If anyone has wanted whip cream
on their pie, it's not my fault.

18:05.505 --> 18:07.873
I can't get a (bleep) word
in edgewise around here.

18:07.941 --> 18:09.909
Kevin, I don't want
to lose you again.

18:09.977 --> 18:11.677
But I do have
a job to do.

18:11.745 --> 18:13.145
So get moving.

18:13.213 --> 18:15.431
'Cause there's at least
one Swanson man in this room

18:15.498 --> 18:16.549
who does his duty.

18:16.633 --> 18:17.717
Doodie means poop.
It does.

18:17.784 --> 18:18.851
Are you, Dad?

18:18.919 --> 18:20.786
Are you the only one
who does his duty?

18:20.854 --> 18:22.321
That's not
how I remember it.

18:22.389 --> 18:23.356
What are you
talking about?

18:23.423 --> 18:24.774
You don't remember, do you?

18:24.841 --> 18:26.609
I was in first grade.

18:26.676 --> 18:28.828
<i>You took me out with you
on one of yo shifts.</i>

18:28.895 --> 18:30.696
I just think it's
presumptuous.

18:30.764 --> 18:32.732
I mean, come on, "the
country's best yogurt"?

18:32.799 --> 18:34.066
You know, prove it.

18:34.134 --> 18:35.434
Show me your data.

18:35.502 --> 18:36.469
MAN:
Help! Police!

18:36.536 --> 18:38.355
That man's a thief!

18:40.158 --> 18:41.525
Go get him, Dad!

18:51.202 --> 18:53.837
Reach for the sky, dirtbag!

18:57.191 --> 18:58.308
What the hell?

18:58.375 --> 18:59.526
I'm sorry, Officer.

18:59.594 --> 19:01.395
I-I was just trying
to feed my family.

19:01.462 --> 19:03.997
I don't care what you were
trying to do, you broke the law.

19:04.065 --> 19:05.015
I know.

19:05.083 --> 19:06.650
I didn't know what else to do.

19:06.718 --> 19:08.985
I have three very wide-eyed
children to feed.

19:10.772 --> 19:13.707
Wide-eyed children
are the hungriest.

19:13.775 --> 19:17.077
Kids, Daddy has to go away
for a while, okay?

19:17.145 --> 19:18.845
Take care of Mommy
until she dies tomorrow.

19:18.913 --> 19:20.247
It's okay, Daddy.

19:20.314 --> 19:22.082
I'll be the man of the shack
while you're gone.

19:22.150 --> 19:24.017
(coughing)

19:26.437 --> 19:29.506
Uh, listen,
there's been a mistake.

19:29.574 --> 19:31.475
I'm not here
to arrest your father.

19:31.542 --> 19:32.275
You're not?

19:32.343 --> 19:33.744
No, Son.

19:33.811 --> 19:36.581
I just came over to tell you
what a great guy your father is

19:36.648 --> 19:38.883
and how hard he's been
working for all of you.

19:38.951 --> 19:40.518
Wait. You're letting me go?

19:40.586 --> 19:42.019
I don't understand.

19:42.087 --> 19:44.122
I'm not sure I do either.

19:44.189 --> 19:46.691
I know the law says
I have to arrest you.

19:46.759 --> 19:49.393
But sometimes the law
is just a piece of paper.

19:49.445 --> 19:51.362
There's right, there's wrong,

19:51.429 --> 19:54.382
and somewhere in between,
there's life.

19:54.450 --> 19:56.284
You folks have a good day.

19:58.320 --> 20:00.621
I never forgot what you did.

20:00.689 --> 20:03.324
Well, thanks for
coming, you guys.

20:03.392 --> 20:05.359
You know what you were
doing that day, Dad?

20:05.427 --> 20:06.828
You were breaking the law.

20:06.895 --> 20:09.781
You knew what your duty told you
you were supposed to do,

20:09.848 --> 20:12.583
but instead, you did
what you thought was right.

20:12.651 --> 20:15.787
I haven't thought about
that family in years.

20:15.854 --> 20:17.388
I think about them
all the time.

20:17.456 --> 20:19.690
And the example
you set for me that day.

20:19.758 --> 20:21.592
It's the very reason
I left Iraq.

20:21.660 --> 20:23.861
It sucks how early
I have to work.

20:23.929 --> 20:24.996
(sighs)

20:25.063 --> 20:27.098
Well, Kevin,
I still can't say

20:27.166 --> 20:29.267
that I agree
with your choice.

20:29.334 --> 20:30.868
Not one bit.

20:30.936 --> 20:32.404
But I suppose
sometimes a man

20:32.472 --> 20:34.206
has to do
what he thinks is right.

20:34.274 --> 20:36.308
And if that's what
you thought was right,

20:36.376 --> 20:37.976
I guess I can accept it.

20:39.245 --> 20:40.646
I'm glad you're
home, son.

20:40.713 --> 20:42.080
Me, too, Dad.

20:44.083 --> 20:46.935
You know, there's still
some pumpkin pie left, everyone.

20:47.003 --> 20:48.203
That sounds
good to me.

20:48.288 --> 20:49.338
Me, too.

20:49.422 --> 20:51.957
Hey, happy Thanksgiving
to us all, huh?

20:52.008 --> 20:54.443
(excited chattering)

20:54.511 --> 20:56.461
USA! USA!

20:56.513 --> 20:57.429
MAN:
Wait!

20:57.497 --> 20:58.931
That man's an imposter.

20:58.998 --> 21:00.833
I'm the real Kevin Swanson!

21:00.900 --> 21:02.301
Guys, I don't think
we have time for this.

21:02.302 --> 22:03.302
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru